{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — Pagination
  Phase 18. One paginator, rendered by every surface that pages.

  Accepts:
  - paginate {Object} required. The paginate object from inside a
                      {% paginate %} block.
  - label    {String} optional. The nav's accessible name. Defaults to the
                      generic one; a surface with a more specific answer — the
                      search page — passes its own.

  Usage:
  {% paginate collection.products by n %}
    ...
    {% render 'pagination', paginate: paginate %}
  {% endpaginate %}

  WHY THIS EXISTS: IT WAS BUILT TWICE.

  main-collection and main-search each had their own paginator, under their own
  class names, in their own stylesheet — and the two had already drifted apart
  in ways a customer could see. Collection used caption type; search used
  uppercase label type at a heavier weight and wider tracking. Collection marked
  the current page with weight and a rule; search marked it with gold. Two
  paginations, one shop.

  section-main-collection.css said in writing what to do about it: "It stays in
  this stylesheet only while this is its single consumer. The moment a second
  surface renders it, the block moves to assets/component-pagination.css." The
  second surface existed; the move had not happened.

  WHICH ONE SURVIVED, AND WHY IT IS A MERGE RATHER THAN A RENAME.

  The two were not the same component with different names. Each was better in
  a different place, and a straight rename would have thrown one of them away:

  - The TYPE is collection's. Caption rather than tracked uppercase label: a
    row of page numbers is metadata, not a set of labels, and 0.22em tracking
    on a single digit reads as a gap rather than as letterspacing.

  - The CURRENT-PAGE MARKER is collection's. Weight plus a rule beneath, never
    gold on its own. Search coloured the current page with the accent, which
    makes the state colour-dependent (SC 1.4.1) even with the border present.

  - The ACCESSIBILITY is search's, in all three places it differs. The gap's
    aria-hidden goes on the <li>, not on the span inside it — hiding only the
    span leaves a list item with no accessible content, which is announced as
    a blank entry and inflates the list count. The current page carries a full
    "Page 4" hidden label with the digit beside it marked decorative, so it is
    not read twice. And links are named with aria-label, which is safe on an
    anchor because an anchor has a role that supports naming, while the current
    page — a bare span — uses hidden text, because aria-label on a role-less
    element is not reliably exposed.

  Phase 16's accessibility audit had already found collection's empty list item
  independently. Consolidating fixes it rather than carrying it to a second
  surface.

  TYPE COERCION: Liquid's == does not coerce.
  paginate.current_page is a number and part.title is a string, so they never
  compare equal without help. Both sides are appended to an empty string before
  comparison, which also makes the gap part — whose title is an ellipsis — fall
  through to the third branch rather than matching by accident.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  comment
    The fallback is resolved in two steps, not with `default`. A filter chain
    applies left to right, so `label | default: 'key' | t` runs the t filter
    over whatever came out of default — including a label the CALLER already
    translated, which comes back as "Translation missing: Search results pages".
    Only the fallback key may be passed to t.
  endcomment
  assign nav_label = label
  if nav_label == blank
    assign nav_label = 'general.pagination.label' | t
  endif

  assign current_title = paginate.current_page | append: ''
-%}

<nav class="pagination" aria-label="{{ nav_label }}">
  <ul class="pagination__list" role="list">
    {%- if paginate.previous.is_link -%}
      <li class="pagination__item">
        <a class="pagination__link" href="{{ paginate.previous.url }}" rel="prev">
          {{- 'general.pagination.previous' | t -}}
        </a>
      </li>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- for part in paginate.parts -%}
      {%- assign part_title = part.title | append: '' -%}
      {%- if part.is_link -%}
        <li class="pagination__item">
          <a
            class="pagination__link"
            href="{{ part.url }}"
            aria-label="{{ 'general.pagination.page_number' | t: number: part.title }}"
          >{{ part.title }}</a>
        </li>
      {%- elsif part_title == current_title -%}
        <li class="pagination__item">
          <span class="pagination__link pagination__link--current" aria-current="page">
            <span class="visually-hidden">
              {{- 'general.pagination.page_number' | t: number: part.title -}}
            </span>
            <span aria-hidden="true">{{ part.title }}</span>
          </span>
        </li>
      {%- else -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          aria-hidden on the LIST ITEM, not on the span inside it. See the
          header note: hiding only the span leaves an item with no accessible
          content and the list announces a blank entry.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <li class="pagination__item" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="pagination__gap">{{ part.title }}</span>
        </li>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}

    {%- if paginate.next.is_link -%}
      <li class="pagination__item">
        <a class="pagination__link" href="{{ paginate.next.url }}" rel="next">
          {{- 'general.pagination.next' | t -}}
        </a>
      </li>
    {%- endif -%}
  </ul>
</nav>
